  "numerical_characteristics": {
    "dtype": "int64",
    "precision": "nanosecond",
    "magnitude": "values near the int64 bound: \"106751 days 106751 days\" wraps to Timedelta('-2 days +00:25:26.290448384'); \"99999999999999999999:00:00\" overflows a C long; \"2562047:47:16.854775808\" is the nanosecond out-of-bounds boundary",
    "tolerance": null,
    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "Before the fix, result was declared int64_t in parse_timedelta_string and parse_iso_format_string, and the term conversion used <int64_t> casts, so large totals silently wrapped; the fix changes result to object (Python int) and removes the cast.",
      "PR reproducer: pd.Timedelta(\"106751 days 106751 days\") returned Timedelta('-2 days +00:25:26.290448384') instead of raising, a silent wraparound with a wrong value.",
      "PR reproducer: pd.Timedelta(\"99999999999999999999:00:00\") raised OverflowError: Python int too large to convert to C long, instead of OutOfBoundsDatetime-style error.",
      "PR reproducer: pd.Timedelta(\"1000000 days\") raises OutOfBoundsDatetime with message \"cannot convert input 1000000.0 with the unit 'D'\".",
      "Test added expects \"Out of bounds nanosecond timedelta\" for total-out-of-range strings such as \"106751 days 106751 days\" and \"2562047:47:16.854775808\", and per-term unit errors such as \"cannot convert input 1e+20 with the unit 'ns'\" for \"99999999999999999999ns\".",
      "Fix adds _check_td_ns(sign*result, ts) as the return value of parse_iso_format_string so the total is range-checked against nanosecond bounds.",
      "Commit notes scalar to_timedelta(errors=\"coerce\") previously caught only ValueError so a bare OverflowError propagated; the test pins that path."
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "The exact out-of-bounds message text depends on the unit path (per-term cast_from_unit versus total check), so expected messages must match the branch that is hit.",
      "The wraparound example depends on int64 arithmetic and would not reproduce on a build where the fix is applied.",
      "Exact error type for the overflow case (OverflowError vs OutOfBoundsDatetime/ValueError) is only stated for pre-fix behavior in the PR reproducer; post-fix type is not stated in the passages."
    ]
  },
